Guided Exercise: Manage Non-shared Storage with Stateful Sets
Deploy a replicated web server by using a deployment and verify that all web server pods share a PV; 
and deploy a replicated MySQL database by using a stateful set and verify that each database instance gets a dedicated PV.
Outcomes
Deploy a web server with persistent storage.
Add data to the persistent storage.
Scale the web server deployment and observe the data that is shared with the replicas.
Create a database server with a headless service and a stateful set by using YAML manifest files.
Verify that each instance from the stateful set has a persistent volume claim.

1️⃣ Goal of the Exercise
You are asked to:
1.	Deploy a replicated web server (stateless) using a Deployment sharing a Persistent Volume (PV).
2.	Deploy a replicated MySQL database using a StatefulSet, ensuring each database pod gets its own PV.
Key difference:
•	Deployment + shared PV: Multiple replicas share the same data.
•	StatefulSet + dedicated PVs: Each replica gets its own storage, keeping unique data.
________________________________________
2️⃣ Step 1: Prepare the Environment
Command:
lab start storage-statefulsets
•	What it does: Sets up the OpenShift project and ensures all lab resources (like storage classes) are ready.
________________________________________
3️⃣ Step 2: Log in to OpenShift Cluster
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
oc project storage-statefulsets
•	oc login: Log in as a user to OpenShift.
•	oc project: Switches to the correct project (namespace) for this lab.
________________________________________
4️⃣ Step 3: Deploy Web Server with Deployment
oc create deployment web-server --image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/hello-world-nginx:latest
•	Deployment: Manages stateless pods. Can scale up/down easily.
•	Web server pods are stateless initially, meaning they don’t store unique data.
Verify deployment:
oc get deployment
•	Shows pods status, replicas, etc.
________________________________________
5️⃣ Step 4: Add a Persistent Volume Claim (PVC) to the Deployment
oc set volumes deployment/web-server \
  --add --name web-pv --type persistentVolumeClaim --claim-mode rwo \
  --claim-size 5Gi --mount-path /usr/share/nginx/html --claim-name web-pv-claim
•	oc set volumes: Attaches a volume to an existing deployment.
•	PVC: Requests persistent storage from the cluster.
•	RWO (ReadWriteOnce): Can be mounted by a single pod at a time—but for NFS, multiple pods can access.
•	Mount path: /usr/share/nginx/html → default web content location for Nginx.
•	Claim name: web-pv-claim → PV gets bound here.
Check pod status:
oc get pods -l app=web-server
•	Ensures deployment recreates pod with the PVC attached.
Verify PVC:
oc get pvc
•	STATUS=Bound → PV successfully provisioned from storage class.
________________________________________
6️⃣ Step 5: Add Data to Shared PVC
oc exec -it pod/<pod-name> -- /bin/bash -c 'echo "Hello, World from ${HOSTNAME}" > /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html'
•	oc exec: Runs commands inside the pod.
•	Adds a file with pod name to show shared storage.
Check file:
oc exec -it pod/<pod-name> -- cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
________________________________________
7️⃣ Step 6: Scale Deployment
oc scale deployment web-server --replicas 2
oc get pods
•	Adds another pod (replica).
•	Both pods share the same PV, so new pod sees the same data as first pod.
Check file on new pod:
oc exec -it pod/<new-pod-name> -- cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
Observation: Both pods show the same data, because the PV is shared.
