8️⃣ Step 7: Deploy Database with StatefulSet
Why StatefulSet?
•	Stateful applications need unique identity and persistent storage.
•	Examples: Databases like MySQL, Cassandra.
•	Each pod gets its own PV, unlike Deployment.
Step 7a: Headless Service
File: service-db.yml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: mysql-svc
spec:
  clusterIP: None
  ports:
  - port: 3306
    name: mysql
  selector:
    app: database
•	Headless Service (clusterIP: None): Allows pods to have stable DNS names, e.g., dbserver-0.mysql-svc.
•	Essential for StatefulSets to reach each pod individually.
Create service:
oc create -f service-db.yml
________________________________________
Step 7b: StatefulSet Definition
File: statefulset-db.yml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: dbserver
spec:
  replicas: 2
  serviceName: mysql-svc
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: database
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: database
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: dbserver
        image: registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/mysql-app:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3306
        env:
        - name: MYSQL_USER
          value: "redhat"
        - name: MYSQL_PASSWORD
          value: "redhat123"
        - name: MYSQL_DATABASE
          value: "sakila"
        volumeMounts:
        - name: data
          mountPath: /var/lib/mysql
  volumeClaimTemplates:
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes: [ "ReadWriteOnce" ]
      storageClassName: "lvms-vg1"
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 1Gi
•	volumeClaimTemplates: Ensures each pod gets its own PVC.
•	Mount path: /var/lib/mysql → MySQL data directory.
•	Each pod is numbered: dbserver-0, dbserver-1.
Create StatefulSet:
oc create -f statefulset-db.yml
Check pods:
watch oc get pod -l app=database -o wide
________________________________________
9️⃣ Step 8: Verify Persistent Storage
oc get pvc -l app=database
•	Shows unique PVC for each pod: data-dbserver-0, data-dbserver-1.
Check PVC mount inside pod:
oc get pod dbserver-0 -o json | jq .spec.volumes[0].persistentVolumeClaim.claimName
•	Confirms pod mounts correct PV.
________________________________________
10️⃣ Step 9: Add Data to Each DB Pod
oc exec -it pod/dbserver-0 -- mysql -uredhat -predhat123 sakila -e 'create table items (count INT);'
oc exec -it pod/dbserver-1 -- mysql -uredhat -predhat123 sakila -e 'create table inventory (count INT);'
•	Each pod stores unique data, no automatic replication.
Check tables:
oc exec -it pod/dbserver-0 -- mysql -uredhat -predhat123 sakila -e 'show tables;'
oc exec -it pod/dbserver-1 -- mysql -uredhat -predhat123 sakila -e 'show tables;'
________________________________________
11️⃣ Step 10: Pod Deletion & PVC Persistence
oc delete pod dbserver-0
oc get pods -l app=database
•	New pod dbserver-0 is recreated with same PVC.
•	Data persists because PVC is not deleted with pod.
________________________________________
✅ Key Takeaways (Theory + Practice)
Concept	Deployment (Web)	StatefulSet (DB)
Pod Identity	Ephemeral, interchangeable	Stable, numbered (-0, -1)
PV	Shared (optional)	Dedicated for each pod
Data	Shared if PV is shared	Unique per pod
DNS/Service	ClusterIP service	Headless service for stable DNS
Use case	Stateless apps (web servers)	Stateful apps (databases)
Commands Summary:
•	oc create deployment → create deployment
•	oc set volumes → attach PVC to deployment
•	oc scale deployment → scale replicas
•	oc create -f → apply YAML (Service, StatefulSet)
•	oc exec -it pod -- <cmd> → execute command inside pod
•	oc get pods/pvc/deployment/statefulset → check resources
•	oc delete pod → delete pod (test PV persistence)

